Here's the table, `mpn_search_cache`.

**Columns**

| Column | Type | Example |
|---|---|---|
| `chat_id` | text | `chat-abc` |
| `manufacturer_norm` | text | `TI` (empty if unknown) |
| `mpn_norm` | text | `LM317T` |
| `result` | jsonb | alternatives list (below) |
| `status` | text | `success` |
| `result_version` | integer | `1` |
| `fetched_at` | timestamptz | `2026-10-14 10:00` |
| `expires_at` | timestamptz | `2026-10-15 10:00` |
| `created_at` | timestamptz | `2026-10-14 10:00` |
| `updated_at` | timestamptz | `2026-10-14 10:00` |

The primary key is `(chat_id, manufacturer_norm, mpn_norm)`. There's an index on `expires_at` for cleanup.

**Sample rows (one chat, 100 MPNs uploaded)**

| chat_id | manufacturer_norm | mpn_norm | status |
|---|---|---|---|
| chat-abc | TI | LM317T | success |
| chat-abc | ST | STM32F407VGT6 | success |
| chat-abc | MICROCHIP | PIC16F877A-I/P | no_alternatives |
| chat-abc | TI | SN74HC595N | success |
| ... | ... | ... | ... |

That is 100 rows for `chat-abc`. Each row also has `result_version` 1 and an `expires_at` 24 hours after `fetched_at`.

**What the `result` column holds for one row**

```json
{
  "alternatives": [
    {"mpn": "LM317TG", "manufacturer": "onsemi", "source_url": "https://...", "confidence": 0.92},
    {"mpn": "L7805CV", "manufacturer": "ST", "source_url": "https://...", "confidence": 0.85}
  ],
  "provider": "web_search_provider",
  "schema_version": 1
}
```

For a part with no alternatives, `status` is `no_alternatives` and `alternatives` is an empty list.

**A different chat**

If another user uploads `LM317T`, it becomes a separate row with its own `chat_id`, such as `chat-xyz`. Chats never see each other's rows.

If your Excel has no manufacturer column, `manufacturer_norm` stays empty for every row and the key effectively becomes `(chat_id, mpn_norm)`.
